Counting the Number of Set Bits :

Approach:

To count the number of set bits (1s) in a number, we can check each bit one by one using: n & 1

The idea is:
n & 1 checks the last bit of the number.
If the result is 1, the last bit is set, so increase the count.
Right shift the number by 1 to check the next bit.

Example:
Given:
n = 13
Binary:
13 = 1101
Now check each bit.

Step 1:

Check last bit:

1101
0001
----
0001

Result:1

Count: count = 1

Right shift:

1101 >> 1
0110
Step 2:
Check last bit:

0110
0001
----
0000

Result:0
Count remains: count = 1

Right shift:

0110 >> 1
0011
Step 3:

Check last bit:

0011
0001
----
0001

Result:1

Count:count = 2

Right shift:

0011 >> 1
0001
Step 4:

Check last bit:

0001
0001
----
0001

Result:1

Count:count = 3

Right shift:

0001 >> 1

0000

Loop stops.

Answer:
13 has 3 set bits.
Why Right Shift?

Right shift (>>) removes the last bit after checking it.

Example:

1101 >> 1 = 0110
0110 >> 1 = 0011
0011 >> 1 = 0001
0001 >> 1 = 0000

The loop continues until all bits are checked.

Code:

class Solution {
public:
    int countSetBits(int n) {
        int count = 0;

        while (n > 0) {
            count += (n & 1);
            n >>= 1;
        }

        return count;
    }
};

Time Complexity: O(log N)
Each iteration checks one bit.
For a number N, the number of bits is approximately log₂(N).

Space Complexity: O(1)
No extra space is used.

💡 Shortcut to remember:

n & 1 → Check the last bit
n >> 1 → Move to the next bit

Process:

Check → Count → Shift → Repeat
